<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Bituriges Cubi</h1><p class="st-meta">400 BCE – 52 BCE (approx.) · capital Avaricum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Powerful Gallic people of Berry whose capital Avaricum, modern Bourges, was stormed by Caesar in one of the bloodiest sieges of the Gallic War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Bituriges, whose name means 'kings of the world', occupied the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cher-river">Cher</a> basins around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaricum">Avaricum</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourges">Bourges</a>). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> tells a legend that in the 6th century BCE their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambigatus">Ambigatus</a> held supremacy over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> and sent his nephews <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bellovesus">Bellovesus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segovesus">Segovesus</a> to lead migrations into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hercynian-forest">Hercynian Forest</a>. A related branch, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bituriges-vivisci">Bituriges Vivisci</a>, later settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their land was rich in iron ore, and the Bituriges were renowned smiths and metalworkers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaricum">Avaricum</a>, defended by a massive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>-laced murus gallicus rampart, was one of the largest and wealthiest oppida in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 52 BCE they joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> and burned some twenty of their own towns to starve the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, but spared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaricum">Avaricum</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a> took after a hard siege, killing most of its inhabitants. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> their territory became a civitas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-aquitania">Aquitania</a>, the origin of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berry-province">Berry</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bituriges-cubi.jpg" alt="Bituriges Cubi" width="320" height="299"><figcaption>Image: The Department of History, United States Military Academy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gaul,_1st_century_BC.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>400 BCE – 52 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Avaricum</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaulish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bituriges</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-226&amp;focus=bituriges-cubi" aria-label="Bituriges Cubi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4736 525 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bituriges Cubi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5056 781L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5056 781h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5056 781h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Avaricum. <a href="/atlas?year=-226&amp;focus=bituriges-cubi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

